The probability histogram of a binomial random variable $X$ is shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.72]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,9.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(9.8,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {2/0.1,4/0.2,6/0.3,8/0.4} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (2.0,0.300);
\draw (2.5,0) rectangle (3.7,2.230);
\draw (4.2,0) rectangle (5.4,6.211);
\draw (5.9,0) rectangle (7.1,7.689);
\draw (7.6,0) rectangle (8.8,3.570);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.4,0) {$0$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.1,0) {$1$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.8,0) {$2$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.5,0) {$3$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (8.2,0) {$4$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.9,4) {Probability};
\node[below] at (4.8,-1.0) {Number of successes $r$};
\node at (4.8,9.1) {Distribution of $X$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
What are the number of trials $n$, and how does $p$ compare with $0.5$?
